  "instruction": "# Cannot reschedule timeout after exit or expiration\n\n```\n>>> import asyncio\n>>> from async_timeout import timeout\n>>> \n>>> async def main():\n...     try:\n...         async with timeout(0.1) as t:\n...             await asyncio.sleep(0.2)  # This will cause timeout to expire\n...     except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n...         print(\"Timeout occurred\")\n...         try:\n...             # Try to shift the timeout after it has expired\n...             t.shift(1.0)\n...             print(\"Shift succeeded unexpectedly\")\n...         except RuntimeError as e:\n...             print(f\"Error: {e}\")  # Should get error about expired timeout\n... \n>>> asyncio.run(main())\nTimeout occurred\nError: invalid state TIMEOUT\n```\n\nThe error message is incorrect. When trying to shift or update a timeout after it has expired or after exiting the context manager, we get a generic \"invalid state\" error instead of the specific error message that explains what went wrong.\n\nPreviously, we would get clear error messages like \"cannot reschedule expired timeout\" or \"cannot reschedule after exit from context manager\", which were much more helpful for debugging.\n\nThis affects both the `shift()` and `update()` methods when called after a timeout has expired or after exiting the context manager.\n\nWorkspace and initial working directory (absolute CWD): /testbed",
